      "raw_completion": "{ \"goal_restatement\": \"Find the maximum integer value of k for which the cubic polynomial P(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - k has three distinct real roots.\",\n\"conditions_complete\": true,\n\"contradictory\": false,\n\"multiple_reasonable_interpretations\": false,\n\"solution_exists\": true,\n\"unique_or_explicit_grading\": true,\n\"label\": \"A\",\n\"confidence\": 0.95,\n\"probability_label_A\": 0.95,\n\"issue_types\": [],\n\"reasoning_summary\": \"The polynomial P(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - k has three distinct real roots if and only if its discriminant is positive. The discriminant of a cubic polynomial ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d is given by \u0394 = 18abcd - 4b^3d + b^2c^2 - 4ac^3 - 27a^2d^2. For P(x), a = 1, b = -3, c = 4, and d = -k. Substituting these values into the discriminant formula, we get \u0394 = 18(1)(-3)(4)(-k) - 4(-3)^3(-k) + (-3)^2(4)^2 - 4(1)(4)^3 - 27(1)^2(-k)^2. Simplifying this expression, we find that \u0394 = 216k + 108k + 144 - 256 - 27k^2. Setting \u0394 > 0, we solve for k and find that the maximum integer value of k for which the polynomial has three distinct real roots is 1.\",\n\"derived_answer\":   \n\n\n\n\n\n  \"derived_answer\"  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n